Call, Apply and bind methods : 
=================================

    In JavaScript, call, apply, and bind are methods that allow you to control the value of the this keyword within a function 
    and also enable you to pass arguments to a function in different ways. These methods are commonly used in object-oriented
    programming and functional programming paradigms.

call 
======
    --> The call method is used to call a function immediately with the given this value and Arguments are passed one by 
    one (individually).

    Use case  :
    -----------
    --> Mostly call is used when want to reuse the same function logic across different contexts(for different objects or services)
    , such as logging or shared utility functions
    Ex : logging function for different services


    ### ✅ Use `call` when:

    * You want to execute immediately
    * Reusing logic across services
    * Context switching

    Call example 
    ----------------

    # 5️⃣ `call` — REAL USE CASE (REUSE LOGIC)

    ### Example: Logging utility reused across services

    function log(prefix, message) {
      console.log(`[${this.module}] ${prefix}: ${message}`);
    }

    const orderContext = { module: "Order" };
    const paymentContext = { module: "Payment" };

    log.call(orderContext, "INFO", "Order created");
    log.call(paymentContext, "ERROR", "Payment failed");
    ```

    ### Output

    ```txt
    [Order] INFO: Order created
    [Payment] ERROR: Payment failed
    ```

    ---

    # 7️⃣ Node.js REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE — DB CONNECTION REUSE


    ```js
    function query(sql) {
      console.log(`${this.dbName}: ${sql}`);
    }

    const mysql = { dbName: "MySQL" };
    const mongo = { dbName: "MongoDB" };

    query.call(mysql, "SELECT * FROM orders");
    query.call(mongo, "db.orders.find()");
    ```


apply : 
==========
    --> apply method is similar to call method, the only difference is that instead of passing argument individually, 
    will pass the argument in a list/array

    use case : 
    ----------
    --> It’s useful when the arguments are already available in array form, such as dynamic data or error lists.

    ---

    ### Why `apply`?

    👉 Arguments already in an **array**



    ### ✅ Use `apply` when:

    * Arguments are already in array
    * Dynamic argument lists
    * Math / validation utilities



    # 6️⃣ `apply` — REAL USE CASE (ARRAY DATA)

    ### Example: Validation errors from DB

    ```js
    function sendErrors(code, message1, message2) {
      console.log(this.service, code, message1, message2);
    }

    const context = { service: "OrderAPI" };

    const errors = [400, "Invalid price", "Invalid quantity"];

    sendErrors.apply(context, errors);
    ```


bind : 
=========
    -->`bind` is used when I want to fix the value of `this` and use the function later.
    --> It does not execute the function immediately. Instead, it returns a new function with `this` permanently bound.”

    **Real use case:**

    --> “In Express, when I pass a controller method as a route handler, I use `bind` so that `this` still points to the controller
     instance.”

    Example explanation (no code needed):

    --> “Without `bind`, `this` becomes undefined inside the controller method.”

    ### 💡 Real use

    * Passing function as callback
    * Express routes
    * setTimeout / setInterval
    * Event listeners
    * Background jobs



    Example bind : 
    ----------------

    class OrderService {
      constructor() {
        this.name = "OrderService";
      }

      createOrder() {
        console.log("Service:", this.name);
      }
    }

    const service = new OrderService();

    // simulate passing method as callback
    setTimeout(service.createOrder, 1000);
    ```

    ### ❌ Output

    Service: undefined


    Why?
    👉 `this` is LOST when method is passed as callback



    ## ✅ FIX USING `bind` (MOST COMMON IN NODE)

    setTimeout(service.createOrder.bind(service), 1000);

    ### ✅ Output

    Service: OrderService
    ---------------------------------------
    # 4️⃣ `bind` — REAL EXPRESS USE CASE (VERY IMPORTANT)

    ### ❌ Without bind (BUG)

    class OrderController {
      constructor(service) {
        this.service = service;
      }

      create(req, res) {
        this.service.createOrder();
        res.send("OK");
      }
    }

    const controller = new OrderController(orderService);

    app.post("/orders", controller.create); // ❌


    Error:

    Cannot read property 'createOrder' of undefined

    --------------------------

    ## ✅ With `bind`
    app.post("/orders", controller.create.bind(controller));


# 8️⃣ call vs apply vs bind (REAL DIFFERENCE)
==========================================

    function demo(a, b) {
      console.log(this.name, a, b);
    }

    const ctx = { name: "NodeJS" };

    demo.call(ctx, 1, 2);        // executes now
    demo.apply(ctx, [1, 2]);    // executes now
    const fn = demo.bind(ctx);  // does NOT execute

    fn(1, 2);                   // executes later




Q. Why not arrow functions instead of bind?
==========================================
    Yes — in many cases, arrow functions can replace bind.But they are NOT the same thing, and each has a different purpose.

    Express calls the function later, and at that time this reference is lost.
    Using bind creates a new function where this is permanently set to the controller, so when Express executes it, 
    this.service works correctly


